WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:02.810
- [Narrator] Lake Titicaca is home

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:06.710
to moveable floating islands
that were created by humans

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.282
and where over 500 people
still reside today.

00:00:10.282 --> 00:00:13.782
(upbeat electronic music)

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.990
Living on not so solid
ground isn't for everyone,

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:23.150
but a small South American
tribe in Peru, known as Uros,

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:26.083
have created and maintained
this unique way of life.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.620
The community is made up of
roughly 70 human-made islands

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.893
and one large central island,
which is the hub for the tribe.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:38.290
They use dried reeds that grow
along the banks of the lake

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:42.213
and shape them into floating
platforms and boats.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.580
These platforms can be moved
from the mainland if required,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.543
which was originally a defense
strategy to escape danger.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.860
The reeds are interwoven densely

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.070
to be about 80 inches thick.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.090
Large logs are attached through the reeds

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.743
and anchored to the river floor.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.490
Over time the reeds tend to rot,

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.903
and so they are replaced
four times each year.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.970
The community is home to
the new and old world.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.560
The reeds are used as medicine
and the flowers drunk as tea.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.330
However, the solar panels are used

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.670
to help run some electrical appliances,

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.403
and the central island is
home to a radio station.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.090
Until the mid-'80s, the islands
were located further out

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.150
in the lake and were
inaccessible by visitors.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.680
However, after a devastating storm,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.620
the tribe as moved closer to land

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.410
and started welcoming tourists

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.511
to experience their way of life.

00:01:33.511 --> 00:01:37.011
(upbeat electronic music)

